\ Spitfire: a Supermarine Spitfire Mk I over central Scotland in 1940, in
\ wireframe. Every armed mission but the flying lesson may be flown in a
\ Hawker Hurricane Mk I instead, chosen on its briefing.
\
\   ./telic examples/spitfire.telic
\
\ The title lists the missions: the Ju 88 raid on the Forth of 16 October
\ 1939, from the runway at RAF Turnhouse, west of Edinburgh; the dogfight
\ off the Farne Islands of 15 August 1940, begun among the Bf 110s escorting
\ KG 26's He 111s to Tyneside, which break toward a Spitfire behind them and
\ turn their nose guns onto one ahead, with five more of 72 Squadron's
\ Spitfires fighting beside the player's; the night raids
\ on Clydebank of 13 March 1941 and on Greenock of 6 May 1941, from a patrol
\ over the Clyde; the photographic reconnaissance of 5 March 1942, the
\ unarmed PR IV from RAF Wick 580 miles to Trondheim to photograph the
\ Tirpitz in Fættenfjord, its film developed and reviewed after landing;
\ a flying lesson from Turnhouse, an instructor in the back seat talking
\ the pupil step by step through the take-off, the propeller's pitch, the
\ climb, trim, turns, the stall, flaps and the landing; air firing over the
\ Forth, live rounds at a drogue towed by a Lysander, the instructor taking
\ the pupil through ranging, astern and quarter attacks and a turning
\ target and assessing every burst; free flight from
\ Turnhouse; and landing practice on the
\ approach to Abbotsinch, in clear air and in fog. Each mission has its
\ weather: the visibility, and cumulus at a share of the sky and a base
\ height, which hide what lies behind them and blind the pilot inside them.
\ The map runs
\ from the Clyde to the Firth of Forth, south-east to the Farne Islands
\ and Tyneside, north up the Great Glen to
\ Inverness and on to Wick, and, across 580 miles of the North Sea placed at
\ their true distance and bearing, round the Trondheimsfjord: Trondheim
\ with Nidaros Cathedral, Munkholmen and its harbour, the U-boat bunker Dora I
\ half built, the Luftwaffe's airfield at Værnes, and the Tirpitz with two
\ destroyers behind their nets in Fættenfjord, over the surveyed hills round
\ the fjord. In Scotland: Edinburgh's castle on its rock, St Giles', the Hub, the Tron,
\ the Scott Monument, the Balmoral Hotel, Waverley and the North Bridge,
\ Calton Hill's monuments, St Mary's Cathedral and Fettes College; the Forth
\ Bridge; Glasgow's University on Gilmorehill, Kelvingrove, Trinity
\ College's towers, the Cathedral and the Necropolis, the City Chambers, the
\ Tolbooth Steeple, the Finnieston and Titan cranes and the shipyards;
\ Stirling, Loch
\ Lomond and Loch Ness, the Pentlands, Ochils and Campsies, and the
\ airfields at Turnhouse, Drem, Abbotsinch and Dalcross.
\
\ Keys: the arrows are the stick (down pulls back, lifting the nose; left and
\ right roll), Z and X the rudder, Q and A the throttle, [ and ] the
\ elevator trim nose-down and nose-up, W and S the propeller finer and
\ coarser, E the boost cut-out, C the cameras on the reconnaissance
\ sortie, T time four and sixteen times as fast, V the film's review after
\ landing, F the flaps, G the
\ undercarriage, B the brakes, space the guns, J bails out (the hood back,
\ then over the side, then the ripcord), in the Hurricane comma and period
\ the rudder bias and R the fuel cock, 1 to 4 look ahead, left, right
\ and behind, P pauses, escape returns to the
\ missions. A gamepad: the left stick is the stick, the shoulders the rudder,
\ the right stick's up and down or the d-pad's the throttle, the right
\ stick's left and right the trim, the stick clicks the propeller (right
\ finer, left coarser), the left trigger the boost cut-out, A or the right
\ trigger the guns, X the flaps, Y the undercarriage, B the brakes, the d-pad
\ left and right turns the view, start pauses and back returns to the
\ missions. On the title, up and down or the d-pad choose a mission, return,
\ start or A opens its briefing (a map of the area, the particulars and the
\ orders), where return, start or A flies it; H or Y shows the keys; escape
\ quits.
\
\ The flight model, its figures and their sources are in
\ examples/spitfire/flight.telic; the ground's heights and Trondheim's
\ harbour in norway-terrain.telic, generated by tools/gen-norway-terrain.py
\ from the AWS Terrain Tiles (Norway terrain data © Kartverket) and from
\ OpenStreetMap;
\ the map in geography.telic, generated by
\ tools/gen-spitfire-map.py from Natural Earth and Wikidata, and Glasgow's,
\ Edinburgh's and Inverness's streets, squares, stations, railways and some
\ buildings' footprints from OpenStreetMap
\ (© OpenStreetMap contributors, ODbL); the scenery in
\ world.telic, cities.telic and shapes.telic; the view in render.telic; the clouds in
\ clouds.telic; the cameras, the film's review and the flak in
\ reconnaissance.telic; the lesson's steps in lesson.telic; the air firing in
\ air-firing.telic; the briefings in briefing.telic; bailing out in
\ parachute.telic; the panel's
\ stroke lettering in lettering.telic; the
\ instruments in
\ cockpit.telic; the aircraft's models, the Spitfire's and the Hurricane's
\ among them, in aircraft.telic; the Spitfire's, the PR IV's and the
\ Hurricane's figures for the flight model in aircraft-types.telic; the
\ sound in sound.telic; the game in game.telic.

"spitfire/geography.telic" load
